WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.310 --> 00:00:07.309
I imagined that when we had traveled as far south as Cape Horn, we would turn west and head into the Pacific Ocean.

2
00:00:07.310 --> 00:00:12.721
But Captain Nemo did nothing of the kind. Instead he continued to head south.

3
00:00:12.722 --> 00:00:17.205
Was he going to the South Pole? That would be sheer madness.

4
00:00:17.206 --> 00:00:21.197
In the meantime Ned had stopped mentioning his escape plans.

5
00:00:21.198 --> 00:00:27.372
I could see that our imprisonment was weighing upon him, and I could feel that rage was burning within him.

6
00:00:27.373 --> 00:00:31.522
When he met the captain, his eyes lit up with suppressed anger.

7
00:00:31.523 --> 00:00:35.014
I feared he might do something that he would later regret.

8
00:00:35.015 --> 00:00:39.530
On the morning of March 14, he and Conseil came to my cabin.

9
00:00:39.531 --> 00:00:47.355
"I have one simple question for you, Professor," said Ned. "How many men do you think are onboard the Nautilus

10
00:00:47.356 --> 00:00:53.498
?" "I don't know, my friend," I replied. "Probably ten men would be enough to run

11
00:00:53.499 --> 00:00:59.014
it." I knew that Ned was thinking about how many men he'd have to overpower to take control of the

12
00:00:59.015 --> 00:01:01.665
submarine. "But you have to consider the other people

13
00:01:01.666 --> 00:01:04.594
too." "What other people?" asked

14
00:01:04.595 --> 00:01:09.438
Ned. "If my suspicions are correct, then the Nautilus houses more than just its

15
00:01:09.440 --> 00:01:15.244
crew. It also serves as a refuge for men like Captain Nemo who have cut all ties with

16
00:01:15.245 --> 00:01:22.228
land." "Perhaps that's true, Master," said Conseil, "but this submarine can hold only a limited number of

17
00:01:22.229 --> 00:01:25.901
men. You know the size of the vessel and the amount of air it

18
00:01:25.902 --> 00:01:31.744
contains. You also know that the Nautilus must return to the surface every 24 hours to refresh its air

19
00:01:31.745 --> 00:01:35.331
supply. Can't you estimate the maximum number of men it could

20
00:01:35.332 --> 00:01:37.543
hold?" I did some quick

21
00:01:37.544 --> 00:01:46.633
calculations. "There's enough air onboard to supply 625 men for 24 hours, but I can't imagine there are more than 60 men onboard."

22
00:01:46.634 --> 00:01:50.315
"That's still too many," said Conseil.

23
00:01:50.316 --> 00:01:54.728
Ned simply shook his head and left my cabin without saying anything.

24
00:01:54.729 --> 00:01:59.799
"Will you allow me to make one observation, Master?" asked Conseil.

25
00:01:59.800 --> 00:02:06.870
"We must try to understand how difficult it is for poor Ned because there's nothing for him to do here.

26
00:02:06.871 --> 00:02:10.180
He's not as interested in the wonders of the sea as we are."

27
00:02:10.181 --> 00:02:18.093
"I agree, Conseil. The monotony of life onboard must seem intolerable to him after a life of freedom and activity."

28
00:02:18.094 --> 00:02:25.688
Ned rarely reacted to anything at sea, but later that morning something happened that seemed to spark a change in him.

29
00:02:25.689 --> 00:02:30.188
The three of us were sitting on the platform, relaxing in the fine weather.

30
00:02:30.189 --> 00:02:38.014
The seasons are reversed in the Southern Hemisphere, which meant we were experiencing fall at a time when we normally enjoyed spring in Europe.

31
00:02:38.015 --> 00:02:42.164
Suddenly Ned jumped up and shouted, "A whale!"

32
00:02:42.165 --> 00:02:48.148
"So," I teased him, "you haven't lost your ability to spot whales after all these months!"

33
00:02:48.149 --> 00:02:57.974
"Never, Professor. Once a whaler, always a whaler. Why, I believe there are ten whales out there. No, wait, twenty!

34
00:02:57.975 --> 00:03:02.164
And I can't go after them because I'm stuck on this confounded vessel."

35
00:03:02.165 --> 00:03:07.569
Ned pounded his fist on the railing, but his eyes looked brighter than they had in weeks.

36
00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:17.227
From that point on, the Nautilus remained on the surface of the ocean, and we soon began to see floating pieces of ice about twenty to twenty-five feet long.

37
00:03:17.228 --> 00:03:22.227
We passed even larger pieces and finally encountered actual icebergs.

38
00:03:22.228 --> 00:03:30.790
Ned had fished in the Arctic, so he was familiar with its icebergs, but Conseil and I admired this natural phenomenon for the first time.

39
00:03:30.791 --> 00:03:39.116
Some of these icebergs were streaked with green; others resembled enormous amethysts with the light shining through them.

40
00:03:39.117 --> 00:03:45.870
The farther south we went, the more these floating islands increased both in number and size.

41
00:03:45.871 --> 00:03:56.926
The ice took surprising forms, sometimes resembling a Middle Eastern town with mosques and minarets, and then, a city tumbled to the ground by an earthquake.

42
00:03:56.927 --> 00:04:04.013
No matter where we were, Captain Nemo always managed to find a small trail of blue water for us to follow.

43
00:04:04.014 --> 00:04:09.513
One day the ice completely blocked our passage, but this didn't stop the captain.

44
00:04:09.514 --> 00:04:15.854
The Nautilus entered the brittle ice like a wedge and split it with a frightful cracking sound.

45
00:04:15.855 --> 00:04:21.521
Violent gales struck us and every part of the submarine was covered with ice.

46
00:04:21.522 --> 00:04:29.910
We traveled on until about two o'clock when, despite all the captain's efforts, the Nautilus could proceed no farther.

47
00:04:29.911 --> 00:04:34.577
We couldn't go backward either because every opening had closed behind us.

48
00:04:34.578 --> 00:04:39.259
I was on the platform with Captain Nemo when we became trapped in the ice.

49
00:04:39.260 --> 00:04:43.656
"Well, Professor, what do you think of this?" he asked.

50
00:04:43.657 --> 00:04:50.823
I wanted to say that I thought he'd been very unwise to get us into this situation, but I restrained myself.

51
00:04:50.824 --> 00:04:54.140
Instead I said, "I think we're stuck, Captain."

52
00:04:54.141 --> 00:04:57.902
"So you don't think the Nautilus can free itself?"

53
00:04:57.903 --> 00:05:03.886
"I think it'll be difficult because winter is coming, and the ice won't be breaking up for a long time."

54
00:05:03.887 --> 00:05:08.177
"Oh, Professor, you always see nothing but obstacles and trouble.

55
00:05:08.178 --> 00:05:14.307
I swear to you that the Nautilus will free itself—and then travel all the way to the South Pole!"

56
00:05:14.308 --> 00:05:20.037
"To the South Pole?" I thought in surprise. No one had ever reached the South Pole.

57
00:05:20.038 --> 00:05:27.712
"I will not fail," said the captain as if he were reading my thoughts. "We'll discover it together, you and I."

58
00:05:27.713 --> 00:05:31.275
"Sure," I said sarcastically.

59
00:05:31.276 --> 00:05:39.442
"There are no obstacles for us. Let's blow up this iceberg, and if that fails, let's give the Nautilus wings to fly over it!" "No,

60
00:05:39.443 --> 00:05:44.029
not over it but under it," said Captain Nemo. "As

61
00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:51.267
I'm sure you know, most of an iceberg is underwater, but that won't hinder us because we can go still deeper. The

62
00:05:51.268 --> 00:05:55.442
only problem is that we must come to the surface for fresh air." "Yes,

63
00:05:55.443 --> 00:06:00.022
but the Nautilus is equipped with a metal battering ram on its bow. If

64
00:06:00.023 --> 00:06:05.601
you attack the ice on a diagonal, perhaps the shock would cause it to break up." "Now

65
00:06:05.602 --> 00:06:09.910
you're thinking like an optimist!" The captain smiled. The

66
00:06:09.911 --> 00:06:13.339
preparations for this daring attempt now began. Air

67
00:06:13.340 --> 00:06:19.601
was pumped into the submarine's reservoirs while ten men climbed along the sides of the Nautilus .

68
00:06:19.602 --> 00:06:28.744
Armed with pickaxes, they broke up the ice around the vessel, which was soon free. We went below and the submarine began to descend.

69
00:06:28.745 --> 00:06:36.323
Once underwater, we traveled at such high speed that I calculated we would reach the South Pole in forty hours.

70
00:06:36.324 --> 00:06:43.720
For most of the night, the novelty of the situation kept us at the gallery windows, but around two o'clock, I went to bed.

71
00:06:43.721 --> 00:06:49.307
I had not seen Captain Nemo since our conversation, but I assumed he was in the pilothouse.

72
00:06:49.308 --> 00:06:55.657
The next morning I returned to the gallery, where the instruments told me that the submarine was slowly ascending.

73
00:06:55.658 --> 00:07:01.036
My heart beat very quickly. Were we going to emerge and return to open waters?

74
00:07:01.037 --> 00:07:10.029
No! A second later a huge shock told me that the Nautilus had struck the bottom of an iceberg, a huge one, judging from the deafening sound.

75
00:07:10.030 --> 00:07:20.959
Several times that day the submarine tried to rise again, and every time it struck the ice, which lay like a thick, impenetrable ceiling above it.

